    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "22 June 1941. Three German army groups, with Finnish, Romanian and other allies, attack along a front of nearly 3,000 km."
      },
      {
        "t": 1.2,
        "text": "Within a week Army Group Centre's panzers close a vast pocket around Minsk."
      },
      {
        "t": 3.5,
        "text": "July: another pocket at Smolensk. The road to Moscow seems open, but Soviet resistance is stiffening."
      },
      {
        "t": 9,
        "text": "Hitler sends Guderian's panzers south to help destroy the Soviet armies defending Kiev."
      },
      {
        "t": 11,
        "text": "8 September: Army Group North cuts Leningrad off by land, with the Finns closing in from the north. The siege will last 872 days."
      },
      {
        "t": 12.3,
        "text": "Mid-September: the pincers close east of Kiev, the largest encirclement in military history."
      },
      {
        "t": 14.5,
        "text": "October: the drive on Moscow resumes. Huge new pockets at Vyazma and Bryansk; Moscow panics."
      },
      {
        "t": 16.5,
        "text": "Autumn rains turn the roads to mud and the advance slows."
      },
      {
        "t": 20,
        "text": "Late November: in freezing cold, German spearheads come within about 30 km of the Kremlin."
      },
      {
        "t": 21,
        "text": "In the south Rostov falls on 21 November and is lost a week later: the first big German retreat."
      },
      {
        "t": 23.5,
        "text": "5 December: fresh Soviet divisions, many from Siberia, counter-attack at Moscow in bitter cold and drive the Germans back."
      }
    ]
  },
