    "captions": [
      {
        "t": 0,
        "text": "Night of 5–6 June 1944. Minesweepers clear lanes across the Channel as the invasion fleet approaches Normandy."
      },
      {
        "t": 0.27,
        "text": "00:16: British glider troops land beside the bridges over the Orne canal and river and seize them within minutes."
      },
      {
        "t": 1,
        "text": "Paratroopers of the US 82nd and 101st Airborne drop behind Utah beach, scattered widely in the dark."
      },
      {
        "t": 4.5,
        "text": "Before dawn, US paratroopers take Sainte-Mère-Église."
      },
      {
        "t": 5.5,
        "text": "Bombers and the guns of hundreds of warships pound the coastal defences."
      },
      {
        "t": 6.5,
        "text": "06:30: the Americans land. At Utah, carried off course, they meet light defences. At Omaha they are pinned down beneath the bluffs."
      },
      {
        "t": 7.1,
        "text": "US Rangers climb the cliffs at Pointe du Hoc to silence guns that turn out to have been moved inland."
      },
      {
        "t": 7.5,
        "text": "About an hour later, with the rising tide, British and Canadian troops land at Gold, Juno and Sword."
      },
      {
        "t": 11,
        "text": "By late morning small groups of Americans fight their way up the bluffs at Omaha."
      },
      {
        "t": 16,
        "text": "21st Panzer, the only German tank division near the beaches, counter-attacks north of Caen."
      },
      {
        "t": 20,
        "text": "Some of its tanks reach the coast near Lion-sur-Mer, between Juno and Sword, but pull back that night."
      },
      {
        "t": 23,
        "text": "By midnight about 156,000 Allied troops are ashore. Caen, the day's main objective, will not fall until July."
      }
    ]
  }
